 # C2: Audience snapshot at append (Tattler layer)
 
-Layer participant/audience snapshot metadata onto records at append time, at the Tattler layer. Reuse TAT-39/TAT-50 audience-snapshot work — do not rebuild.
-
-**Snapshot envelope design:**
-
-Current Chorus message shapes (from tattler-protocol.ts):
-- ChorusRoomPostV0: { id, roomId, from, broker, message } — broker is ChorusReplyRouteV0 { name, socketPath, shortId }
-- ChorusRoomDeliveryV0: { id, roomId, from, message, postId }
-- TattlerAgentMessageV0: { id, from, message }
-
-Fields to strip from durable record (ephemeral):
-- broker.socketPath (session-bound, not durable)
-- broker.shortId (session-bound, not durable)
-
-Fields to keep:
-- id (UUIDv7 post id → becomes the durable postId)
-- roomId (stable room UUID)
-- from (display handle at post time — snapshot, not FK)
-- message (body)
-
-Fields to add for durability:
-- agentId (stable Tattler ID FK — the durable author key)
-- seq (SQLite rowid, assigned by the DB on insert — cursor range key)
-- roomName (snapshot of room name at post time)
-- audience: array of { agentId, handle } snapshots at append time (from TAT-39/TAT-50)
-- timestamp (ISO 8601, set at append time)
-
-**TAT-39/50 integration contract:**
-- TAT-39 (participant metadata) stamps ChorusRoomPostV0 with audience at fanout time
-- TAT-50 (broker-owned snapshot) ensures the broker populates the participant list, not the agent
-- C2 consumes this by persisting the audience snapshot into the durable record at append time
-- The durable record stores audience as a JSON array in SQLite (not a separate table — it's a point-in-time snapshot, not a relationship)
-
-**SQLite schema addition:**
-The messages table gets an  TEXT column (JSON array of {agentId, handle} objects). This is denormalized by design — it's a snapshot, not a join target.
-
-Depends on C1 (TAT-71) — the append path and schema must exist before attaching audience snapshots. Design work (this ticket) is parallelizable with C1; implementation is sequential.
-
